/*
 * Linker version script: export nothing from the executable.
 *
 * WHY
 * ---
 * libpqxx declares its type-name registry as a NON-inline variable template
 * (pqxx/strconv.hxx):
 *
 *     template<typename TYPE>
 *     std::string const type_name{internal::demangle_type_name(typeid(TYPE).name())};
 *
 * Every TU that touches a pqxx conversion instantiates it. GCC 15 emits those
 * instantiations into the executable's dynamic symbol table, where they PREEMPT
 * the identical definitions inside libpqxx.so. One object then has two owners:
 * libpqxx.so's initialiser constructs it and ours does too, and at exit both
 * DSOs' finalisers free it -- "free(): double free detected in tcache 2" from
 * __cxa_finalize, via _dl_call_fini.
 *
 * GCC 14 does not export those symbols, so each DSO keeps its own copy and
 * nothing double-frees. That is the only reason production has been fine: it is
 * luck, not design, and it evaporates on a compiler upgrade.
 *
 * libpqxx knows about this class of bug -- see the comment above
 * PQXX_DECLARE_ENUM_CONVERSION in strconv.hxx, where they stopped inlining
 * type_name<ENUM> after a "double free on program exit ... with gcc 12". The
 * built-in specialisations still have the problem.
 *
 * Reproduces in three lines with GCC 15:
 *     #include <pqxx/pqxx>
 *     int main() { return pqxx::type_name<int>.size(); }
 *
 * WHAT THIS DOES
 * --------------
 * Marks every symbol in the executable local, so nothing it defines can preempt
 * a shared library's own definition. An executable has no reason to export
 * anything: mkpool has no plugins and nothing dlopen()s it.
 *
 * Verified: cross-DSO exception matching still works -- a pqxx::broken_connection
 * thrown inside libpqxx.so is still caught by its exact type here, which
 * db_worker.cpp relies on for reconnects.
 */
{
  local: *;
};
